  "content": "% Methodology section for Carbon-Aware Adaptive Routing Protocol paper\n\n\\subsection{System Architecture Overview}\n\nThe proposed Carbon-Aware Adaptive Routing Protocol is built on a two-layer architecture designed to effectively integrate real-time traffic information with carbon emissions data. Figure \\ref{fig:framework} illustrates the overall framework of our approach, which consists of a Link Layer and a Transport Layer working in tandem to optimize routing decisions.\n\n\\begin{figure}[!t]\n\\centering\n\\includegraphics[width=0.9\\linewidth]{framework_diagram.png}\n\\caption{Framework of the proposed Carbon-Aware Adaptive Routing Protocol. The two-layer architecture consists of: (1) Link Layer for managing autonomous logistics areas and their interconnections, and (2) Transport Layer for handling routing decisions based on carbon metrics and real-time traffic information.}\n\\label{fig:framework}\n\\end{figure}\n\nThe Link Layer is responsible for the organization of autonomous logistics areas (ALAs) and their interconnections, incorporating real-time traffic data collection points. It establishes and maintains the physical infrastructure representation, enabling the system to monitor current traffic conditions across the network. The Transport Layer, building upon this foundation, handles the routing decisions by processing carbon metrics alongside the traffic information provided by the Link Layer. This layered approach ensures modularity and separation of concerns, with the Link Layer focusing on network representation and data collection, while the Transport Layer concentrates on decision-making and route optimization.\n\n\\subsection{Carbon Emission Calculation Model}\n\nA critical component of the proposed system is the carbon emission calculation model, which estimates the environmental impact of different routes based on multiple factors. Our model addresses the limitations of existing approaches by considering not only static factors like distance and vehicle type but also dynamic elements such as traffic conditions and vehicle speed.\n\n\\subsubsection{Emission Factor Framework}\n\nThe carbon emission calculation is based on a comprehensive framework that accounts for various factors affecting vehicle emissions. For each vehicle type $v$, we define the following parameters:\n\\begin{itemize}\n    \\item $E_{empty}(v)$: Base emissions (kg CO$_2$/km) when the vehicle is empty\n    \\item $E_{load}(v)$: Additional emissions (kg CO$_2$/km/ton) per ton of load\n    \\item $S(v, s)$: Speed efficiency curve that maps speed $s$ to efficiency multiplier\n\\end{itemize}\n\nThe emission calculation for a specific road segment between nodes $i$ and $j$ is formulated as:\n\n\\begin{equation}\nE_{i,j} = [E_{empty}(v) + E_{load}(v) \\times L] \\times d_{i,j} \\times S(v, s_{i,j})\n\\end{equation}\n\nwhere:\n\\begin{itemize}\n    \\item $E_{i,j}$: Total emissions for segment $(i,j)$ in kg CO$_2$\n    \\item $L$: Load weight in tons\n    \\item $d_{i,j}$: Distance of segment $(i,j)$ in kilometers\n    \\item $s_{i,j}$: Average speed on segment $(i,j)$ in km/h, calculated as $s_{i,j} = \\frac{d_{i,j}}{t_{i,j}} \\times 60$ (converting minutes to hours)\n    \\item $t_{i,j}$: Travel time for segment $(i,j)$ in minutes\n\\end{itemize}\n\nThe speed efficiency multiplier $S(v, s_{i,j})$ captures the non-linear relationship between vehicle speed and fuel efficiency. This relationship typically forms a U-shaped curve, with optimal efficiency at moderate speeds and reduced efficiency at both very low and very high speeds. For speeds between discrete points in the efficiency curve, we employ linear interpolation.\n\n\\begin{equation}\nS(v, s) = S(v, s_1) + \\frac{s - s_1}{s_2 - s_1} \\times [S(v, s_2) - S(v, s_1)]\n\\end{equation}\n\nwhere $s_1$ and $s_2$ are the closest defined speed points such that $s_1 \\leq s \\leq s_2$.\n\n\\subsubsection{Vehicle-Specific Emission Profiles}\n\nOur model incorporates emission profiles for different vehicle types, including conventional and electric vehicles. For each vehicle type, we define specific emission parameters based on empirical data and industry standards. For instance:\n\n\\begin{table}[!t]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Emission Parameters for Different Vehicle Types}\n\\begin{tabular}{|l|c|c|c|}\n\\hline\n\\textbf{Vehicle Type} & \\textbf{$E_{empty}$ (kg CO$_2$/km)} & \\textbf{$E_{load}$ (kg CO$_2$/km/ton)} & \\textbf{Optimal Speed (km/h)} \\\\\n\\hline\nLight Truck & 0.25 & 0.15 & 50-60 \\\\\nMedium Truck & 0.45 & 0.12 & 50-60 \\\\\nHeavy Truck & 0.75 & 0.08 & 50-60 \\\\\nElectric Van & 0.05 & 0.03 & 40-50 \\\\\n\\hline\n\\end{tabular}\n\\label{table:emission_params}\n\\end{table}\n\nFor electric vehicles, emissions are primarily indirect, resulting from electricity generation. We use a grid emission factor (GEF) to convert electricity consumption to equivalent CO$_2$ emissions, accounting for the regional energy mix.\n\n\\subsection{Real-Time Traffic Information Integration}\n\nA key innovation of our approach is the integration of real-time traffic information into the carbon emission calculations and routing decisions. This integration addresses the dynamic nature of transportation networks and enables more accurate emission estimations.\n\n\\subsubsection{Traffic Data Collection and Processing}\n\nThe system collects traffic data from multiple sources, including:\n\\begin{itemize}\n    \\item Road sensors and traffic cameras\n    \\item GPS data from vehicles in the network\n    \\item Historical traffic patterns and predictions\n\\end{itemize}\n\nThis data is processed to create a dynamic representation of current network conditions. For each edge $(i,j)$ in the network, we define a traffic multiplier $T_{i,j}(t)$ at time $t$, which represents the ratio of current travel time to baseline travel time:\n\n\\begin{equation}\nT_{i,j}(t) = \\frac{t_{i,j}(t)}{t_{i,j}^{base}}\n\\end{equation}\n\nwhere $t_{i,j}(t)$ is the current travel time with traffic conditions at time $t$, and $t_{i,j}^{base}$ is the baseline travel time under free-flow conditions.\n\n\\subsubsection{Hourly Traffic Pattern Model}\n\nTo account for predictable traffic variations throughout the day, we implement an hourly traffic pattern model. For each hour $h$ of the day (0-23), we define a base multiplier $M_h$ that represents the typical congestion level. This is combined with a random variation factor $\\delta$ to account for day-to-day fluctuations:\n\n\\begin{equation}\nT_{i,j}(h) = M_h \\times (1 + \\delta)\n\\end{equation}\n\nwhere $\\delta \\in [-\\alpha, \\alpha]$ is a random variation with maximum magnitude $\\alpha$ (typically 0.2, representing ±20\\% variation).\n\n\\subsubsection{Traffic Change Detection}\n\nThe system continuously monitors traffic conditions and detects significant changes that may warrant route recalculation. A significant change is detected when:\n\n\\begin{equation}\n|T_{i,j}(t) - T_{i,j}(t-\\Delta t)| > \\tau\n\\end{equation}\n\nwhere $\\Delta t$ is the monitoring interval and $\\tau$ is the threshold for significant change (typically 0.3 or 30\\%).\n\n\\subsection{Adaptive Routing Algorithm}\n\nThe core of our system is the Carbon-Aware Adaptive Routing Algorithm, which dynamically balances carbon emissions and travel time considerations while adapting to changing traffic conditions.\n\n\\subsubsection{Multi-Criteria Decision Framework}\n\nThe algorithm employs a multi-criteria decision framework that combines carbon emissions and travel time objectives. For each edge $(i,j)$ in the network, we calculate a combined weight $W_{i,j}$ as:\n\n\\begin{equation}\nW_{i,j} = (1 - \\omega) \\times \\frac{t_{i,j}}{\\max_{(a,b) \\in E} t_{a,b}} + \\omega \\times \\frac{E_{i,j}}{\\max_{(a,b) \\in E} E_{a,b}}\n\\end{equation}\n\nwhere:\n\\begin{itemize}\n    \\item $\\omega \\in [0,1]$ is the carbon weight parameter, controlling the balance between time and emissions\n    \\item $t_{i,j}$ is the current travel time for edge $(i,j)$\n    \\item $E_{i,j}$ is the calculated carbon emissions for edge $(i,j)$\n    \\item $\\max_{(a,b) \\in E} t_{a,b}$ and $\\max_{(a,b) \\in E} E_{a,b}$ are normalization factors\n\\end{itemize}\n\nThe carbon weight parameter $\\omega$ can be adjusted based on shipment priorities, with higher values prioritizing carbon reduction and lower values prioritizing delivery time.\n\n\\subsubsection{Route Calculation Algorithm}\n\nAlgorithm \\ref{alg:carbon_aware_adaptive} presents the pseudocode for the Carbon-Aware Adaptive Routing Algorithm. The algorithm creates a weighted graph based on the multi-criteria decision framework and finds the shortest path using Dijkstra's algorithm.\n\n\\begin{algorithm}\n\\caption{Carbon-Aware Adaptive Routing Algorithm}\n\\label{alg:carbon_aware_adaptive}\n\\begin{algorithmic}[1]\n\\REQUIRE Graph $G = (V, E)$, origin node $o$, destination node $d$, vehicle information $v$, carbon weight $\\omega$\n\\ENSURE Route $R$ from $o$ to $d$\n\\STATE Create multi-objective weighted graph $G' = (V, E)$\n\\STATE Initialize empty arrays $times$ and $emissions$ for normalization\n\\FOR{each edge $(i,j) \\in E$}\n    \\STATE $t_{i,j} \\gets$ GetCurrentTravelTime$(i, j)$ \\COMMENT{Get time with traffic}\n    \\STATE Append $t_{i,j}$ to $times$\n    \\STATE $s_{i,j} \\gets \\frac{d_{i,j}}{t_{i,j}} \\times 60$ \\COMMENT{Calculate speed in km/h}\n    \\STATE $E_{i,j} \\gets$ CalculateEmissions$(i, j, v, s_{i,j})$\n    \\STATE Append $E_{i,j}$ to $emissions$\n\\ENDFOR\n\\STATE $t_{max} \\gets \\max(times)$ \\COMMENT{Normalization factors}\n\\STATE $E_{max} \\gets \\max(emissions)$\n\\FOR{each edge $(i,j) \\in E$}\n    \\STATE $t_{norm} \\gets \\frac{t_{i,j}}{t_{max}}$ \\COMMENT{Normalize values to [0,1]}\n    \\STATE $E_{norm} \\gets \\frac{E_{i,j}}{E_{max}}$\n    \\STATE $W_{i,j} \\gets (1 - \\omega) \\times t_{norm} + \\omega \\times E_{norm}$ \\COMMENT{Combined weight}\n    \\STATE Set edge weight of $(i,j)$ in $G'$ to $W_{i,j}$\n\\ENDFOR\n\\STATE $R \\gets$ ShortestPath$(G', o, d)$ \\COMMENT{Using Dijkstra's algorithm}\n\\RETURN $R$\n\\end{algorithmic}\n\\end{algorithm}\n\n\\subsubsection{Dynamic Route Adaptation}\n\nThe system continuously monitors traffic conditions and recalculates routes when significant changes are detected. Algorithm \\ref{alg:route_adaptation} outlines the dynamic route adaptation process.\n\n\\begin{algorithm}\n\\caption{Dynamic Route Adaptation}\n\\label{alg:route_adaptation}\n\\begin{algorithmic}[1]\n\\REQUIRE Current route $R$, vehicle information $v$, carbon weight $\\omega$, monitoring interval $\\Delta t$\n\\ENSURE Updated route $R'$\n\\WHILE{route not completed}\n    \\STATE Wait for $\\Delta t$ time units\n    \\STATE $changes \\gets$ DetectTrafficChanges$(R)$\n    \\IF{$changes > threshold$}\n        \\STATE $current \\gets$ GetCurrentPosition$()$\n        \\STATE $R' \\gets$ CarbonAwareAdaptiveRouting$(current, destination, v, \\omega)$\n        \\STATE Update route to $R'$\n    \\ENDIF\n\\ENDWHILE\n\\end{algorithmic}\n\\end{algorithm}\n\n\\subsection{Multi-Criteria Decision Making Process}\n\nThe multi-criteria decision making (MCDM) process in our system balances multiple competing objectives, primarily carbon emissions reduction and travel time minimization, while considering additional constraints such as delivery deadlines and vehicle characteristics.\n\n\\subsubsection{Objective Function}\n\nThe MCDM problem is formulated as minimizing a weighted objective function:\n\n\\begin{equation}\n\\min_{\\text{route } R} \\left[ \\omega \\times \\frac{E(R)}{E_{base}} + (1 - \\omega) \\times \\frac{T(R)}{T_{base}} \\right]\n\\end{equation}\n\nsubject to:\n\\begin{align}\nT(R) \\leq T_{max} \\quad &\\text{(delivery time constraint)} \\\\\nE(R) \\leq E_{max} \\quad &\\text{(emissions constraint)}\n\\end{align}\n\nwhere:\n\\begin{itemize}\n    \\item $E(R)$ is the total carbon emissions for route $R$\n    \\item $T(R)$ is the total travel time for route $R$\n    \\item $E_{base}$ is the emissions of the shortest-distance route (normalization factor)\n    \\item $T_{base}$ is the travel time of the fastest route (normalization factor)\n    \\item $T_{max}$ is the maximum allowable travel time\n    \\item $E_{max}$ is the maximum allowable emissions\n\\end{itemize}\n\n\\subsubsection{Adaptive Carbon Weight Selection}\n\nThe carbon weight parameter $\\omega$ can be dynamically adjusted based on shipment priority and delivery constraints. For time-critical deliveries, a lower $\\omega$ value prioritizes faster routes, while for eco-friendly deliveries, a higher $\\omega$ value prioritizes lower emissions.\n\nThe adaptive carbon weight function is defined as:\n\n\\begin{equation}\n\\omega = \\max\\left(\\min\\left(\\omega_{base} + \\frac{T_{max} - T_{now}}{T_{max}} \\times \\beta, 1\\right), 0\\right)\n\\end{equation}\n\nwhere:\n\\begin{itemize}\n    \\item $\\omega_{base}$ is the default carbon weight\n    \\item $T_{max}$ is the delivery deadline\n    \\item $T_{now}$ is the current time\n    \\item $\\beta$ is an adjustment factor\n\\end{itemize}\n\nThis function increases the carbon weight (prioritizing emissions reduction) when delivery time constraints are loose and decreases it as the deadline approaches.\n\n\\subsection{Implementation and Integration}\n\nThe Carbon-Aware Adaptive Routing Protocol is implemented as an integrated system that combines the components described above. The implementation follows a modular architecture that allows for flexibility and future enhancements.\n\n\\subsubsection{System Components}\n\nOur implementation consists of three primary modules:\n\n\\begin{enumerate}\n    \\item \\textbf{Network Simulator}: Manages the transportation network representation and traffic conditions. It handles node and link management, traffic pattern simulation, and travel time calculations between network points.\n    \n    \\item \\textbf{Carbon Calculator}: Implements the carbon emission calculation model. It maintains vehicle emission profiles, calculates segment-specific emissions based on vehicle parameters and traffic conditions, and compares emissions across different vehicle types.\n    \n    \\item \\textbf{Routing Algorithms}: Implements the four routing algorithms compared in this study: Baseline Shortest Path, Static Carbon-Aware, Traffic-Adaptive, and Carbon-Aware Adaptive.\n\\end{enumerate}\n\n\\subsubsection{Integration with CPI Framework}\n\nThe proposed system integrates with the broader Cyber-Physical Internet (CPI) framework as described by Wu et al. \\cite{cpilogistics2023}. This integration leverages the CPI infrastructure to collect real-time data and execute routing decisions. Figure \\ref{fig:framework} illustrates how our carbon-aware adaptive routing protocol fits within the two-layer CPI architecture.\n\nThe Link Layer of the CPI framework provides the network organization and physical infrastructure representation, while our carbon-aware adaptive routing protocol operates at the Transport Layer, making routing decisions based on the combined consideration of carbon emissions and real-time traffic data.\n\n\\subsection{Summary}\n\nThe methodology presented in this section establishes the foundation for a carbon-aware adaptive routing protocol that effectively balances environmental impact and delivery efficiency. By integrating real-time traffic information with carbon emission calculations, the proposed system can dynamically adjust routes to minimize carbon emissions while respecting time constraints.\n\nThe key innovations of our approach include:\n\\begin{itemize}\n    \\item A comprehensive carbon emission calculation model that accounts for vehicle type, load, speed, and traffic conditions\n    \\item Real-time traffic information integration for accurate emission estimations\n    \\item A multi-criteria decision framework that balances carbon emissions and travel time\n    \\item Dynamic route adaptation in response to changing traffic conditions\n    \\item Adaptive carbon weight selection based on delivery priorities\n\\end{itemize}\n\nThese components work together to create a routing system that is both environmentally conscious and operationally efficient. The next section will describe the experimental setup used to evaluate the effectiveness of the proposed system.",
